arm64: dts: qcom: apq8053-lenovo-cd-18781y: enable the VCNL4200

With the driver measuring proximity on demand (0601), the IR LED fires only
for a proximity read, so the node can be enabled. The IRED on this board is
driven through an external FET (LED_I makes no difference to the counts:
measured at 50, 120 and 200 mA), so the LED current is the board's, and what
userspace sets is pulses, pulse width and read rate.

Also name the I/O supply as the binding and the driver do (vio-supply, not
vddio-supply); with the old name the driver took a dummy regulator for it.

diff --git i/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/apq8053-lenovo-cd-18781y.dts w/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/apq8053-lenovo-cd-18781y.dts
index e0e137610..c16cee380 100644
--- i/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/apq8053-lenovo-cd-18781y.dts
+++ w/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/apq8053-lenovo-cd-18781y.dts
@@ -100,7 +100,7 @@ light-sensor@51 {
 			compatible = "vishay,vcnl4200";
 			reg = <0x51>;
 			vdd-supply = <&pm8953_l10>;
-			vddio-supply = <&pm8953_l6>;
+			vio-supply = <&pm8953_l6>;
 
 			interrupt-parent = <&tlmm>;
 			interrupts = <43 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
@@ -110,12 +110,12 @@ light-sensor@51 {
 			pinctrl-names = "default";
 
 			/*
-			 * WARNING: Do not enable for now!
-			 * There's a chance that this can burn out the sensor's IR LED.
-			 * The sensor turns on the IR LED at high power (so that it
-			 * gets noticeably warm) and never turns it off.
+			 * The IR LED is driven through an external FET, so its
+			 * current is set by the board, not by LED_I. The driver
+			 * pulses it only for a proximity read (active force mode)
+			 * unless a proximity event is enabled, which then runs it
+			 * continuously: do not enable proximity events here.
 			 */
-			status = "disabled";
 		};
 	};
 
